Question 1: Routing & Path Selection (IP Connectivity)A router has received a packet destined for IP address 192.168.10.45. The router's routing table contains the following four routes. Which route will the router select to forward the packet?A) 192.168.10.0/24 via GigabitEthernet0/0B) 192.168.10.32/28 via GigabitEthernet0/1C) 192.168.10.40/29 via GigabitEthernet0/2D) 0.0.0.0/0 via GigabitEthernet0/3Correct Answer: C Explanation: Cisco routers always choose the route with the longest prefix match (the most specific subnet mask) that contains the destination IP address.192.168.10.45 falls into the range of options A, B, and C.Comparing the masks: /29 (Option C) is more specific than /28 (Option B) and /24 (Option A). Therefore, Option C is selected.Question 2: OSI Model & Protocols (Network Fundamentals)Which of the following protocols operates at the Transport Layer (Layer 4) of the OSI model and provides connectionless, unreliable data delivery?A) TCPB) UDPC) ICMPD) ARPCorrect Answer: B Explanation: Both TCP and UDP operate at Layer 4 (Transport). However, UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is connectionless and does not guarantee delivery, making it "unreliable" but fast. TCP is connection-oriented and reliable. ICMP and ARP operate at lower layers (Network and Data Link, respectively).  Question 3: Switching Loops (Network Access)What is the primary purpose of Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) in a Layer 2 switched network?A) To segment a network logically into multiple broadcast domains.  B) To dynamically assign IP addresses to client machines.C) To prevent broadcast storms and MAC table instability caused by redundant physical loops.D) To route packets between different subnets.Correct Answer: C Explanation: Redundant links between switches are great for backup, but they create physical loops. Without STP, broadcast frames would loop endlessly, causing a "broadcast storm" and crashing the network. STP logically blocks redundant paths until they are needed.  Question 4: IP Services & Security (Security Fundamentals)An administrator needs to configure an Access Control List (ACL) to block only web traffic (HTTP) coming from a specific subnet while allowing all other traffic. Which type of ACL should be used, and where should it ideally be applied?A) Standard ACL, applied as close to the source as possible.B) Extended ACL, applied as close to the source as possible.C) Standard ACL, applied as close to the destination as possible.D) Extended ACL, applied as close to the destination as possible.Correct Answer: B Explanation: A Standard ACL can only filter based on the source IP address (which would block all traffic from that subnet, not just HTTP). An Extended ACL can filter by source, destination, protocol, and port number (TCP port 80 for HTTP). Cisco recommends placing Extended ACLs as close to the source as possible to avoid wasting network bandwidth on traffic that will eventually be dropped.Question 5: Infrastructure & Automation (Automation & Programmability)Which of the following data serialization formats is commonly used by REST APIs to manage network infrastructure automation and is characterized by its use of key-value pairs?A) XMLB) JSONC) HTMLD) YAMLCorrect Answer: B Explanation: While YAML and XML are also used in automation, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the most widely tested data serialization format on the CCNA exam for interacting with modern Cisco DNA Center or software-defined controllers via REST APIs. It uses clean, human-readable key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces {}.
